+//! The two numbers a query is wrong about while every other test passes: how a sum rounds, and
+//! what a day is.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.12-aggregation-correctness`. `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` decided *what a
+//! window is* and `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` decided *which records an answer may read*. Both of
+//! them are about records and time, and both of them are wrong about two things neither of those
+//! steps can see: arithmetic that loses the small terms, and a calendar that is not the one the
+//! absolute grid assumes.
+//!
+//! # A sum is not `+`
+//!
+//! Adding a hundred thousand values in the order they arrived loses the small ones to the large
+//! ones: an `f64` carries about sixteen decimal digits, an accumulator over a store's worth of
+//! samples reaches magnitudes where a single sample falls below the last place, and every one of
+//! those additions rounds. [`naive`] is kept in this module **precisely because it is the
+//! implementation everybody writes** — it is what this step's cases prove wrong, and a module
+//! that shipped only the correct version would leave a reader wondering what the fuss is about.
+//!
+//! [`Sum`] is a compensated accumulator (Kahan's rule, in Neumaier's form): it keeps the part of
+//! each addition that the rounding threw away and adds it back at the end. [`BOUND`] is the error
+//! this project promises, stated relative to the **sum of the magnitudes** it added — a scale,
+//! not a fingerprint, because a sum's error is a property of the sizes that went into it rather
+//! than of the answer it produced. [`error_bound`] is that promise as a number a case can ask
+//! for, and [`agrees`] is the comparison for magnitudes that
+//! `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s tolerance makes for values.
+//!
+//! # An answer may not depend on the order the store reads it in
+//!
+//! Segments are read oldest first, a merge rewrites them, and a crash can change an epoch without
+//! reordering anything — so the same records arrive in a different order on a different day, and
+//! **the same question has to answer the same way**. Plain summation does not: the numbers added
+//! first are the ones that stop mattering. Compensation is what makes an answer a function of the
+//! records rather than of the read, and this step's spec shuffles one workload and demands one
+//! answer within [`error_bound`] — and then shows [`naive`] failing the same demand, which is the
+//! case that justifies this module existing at all.
+//!
+//! This is also the honest reading of *monotonic time*. A store's data is full of clocks that
+//! stepped backwards: a machine that corrected its time, a host that was rebooted, a counter that
+//! was replayed. Nothing here may assume that a later record carries a later timestamp, so a
+//! bucket is computed from the records whose timestamp falls in it and never from where a record
+//! sat in the read.
+//!
+//! # A count is an integer
+//!
+//! [`Sum::count`] counts the values that were added, exactly, in a `u64`. `Bucket::count` —
+//! `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s contract — is a `u64` for the same reason: a count carried in an
+//! `f64` is exact only up to two to the fifty-third, and a float count is the bug a store with a
+//! hundred million records finds. A mean is a sum divided by a count, and the division is the
+//! part that is allowed to round — not the count.
+//!
+//! # A day is not twenty-four hours
+//!
+//! Every bucket this store has computed so far sits on the absolute grid, counted from the Unix
+//! epoch, and that grid is the right one for a window of a second, a minute or an hour: an hour
+//! is an hour everywhere. A **day** is not, and the step that says "one point a day" is where a
+//! dashboard built on the absolute grid is a day out, twice a year, in every zone that has a
+//! summer.
+//!
+//! [`Zone`] is a name and the offset transitions **this build knows about**, and the word to
+//! notice is *this build*. A time-zone database is a file that goes stale, ships as a data update
+//! and is wrong the moment a government changes a rule; this project ships the transitions it was
+//! built with and says so. Three consequences a reader should be able to point at:
+//!
+//! * [`Zone::SHIPPED`] is the list of names this build has tables for, and [`Zone::parse`] answers
+//!   those names and a fixed `±HH:MM` offset — nothing else, because a name nobody shipped is a
+//!   name this build cannot be right about;
+//! * [`Zone::offset_at`] answers `None` **outside the table** rather than guessing. A table whose
+//!   rules begin in 2024 cannot say what 2019 was, and inventing `+01:00` for it would be a
+//!   confident answer to a question this build has no data for;
+//! * [`Zone::local_day`] is the half-open span of the *local* day an instant falls in — which is
+//!   twenty-three hours on the day a zone springs forward and twenty-five on the day it falls
+//!   back, and both of those are days a dashboard counting in fixed 86 400-second steps draws
+//!   wrong.
+//!
+//! **The tolerance is a number in the code, and so is the bound.** `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`
+//! states one tolerance for a downsampled answer against a raw one; this step states a second,
+//! different one for a sum against the exact result, and neither of them is a comment: every
+//! comparison of two computed numbers in this step's spec goes through [`agrees`], and the cases
+//! that matter are the ones that **fail** it.
+
+use std::borrow::Cow;
+
+use crate::engine::query::lex::Refusal;
+
+/// How many milliseconds a day is when a caller counts in days on the absolute grid.
+///
+/// The grid this step's zone arithmetic departs from, and the width that makes it depart: a window
+/// of exactly one day is the one window whose buckets a time zone may move (the command line's
+/// `--zone` rule), and every shorter window is already on the absolute grid
+/// `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` pinned.
+pub const DAY_MILLIS: u64 = 86_400_000;
+
+/// The error a compensated sum is allowed, relative to the sum of the magnitudes it added.
+///
+/// The promise is a **relative** one on purpose, and the base is `Σ|v|` rather than the sum
+/// itself: a sum that cancels — `1e16 - 1e16 + 1` — has a tiny answer and a large error budget,
+/// while the same values all positive have a large answer, and a bound stated against the answer
+/// would call the first one exact and the second one broken. What a compensated step cannot cancel
+/// is the rounding of the addition it just made and of the correction it stores; four units in the
+/// last place of the magnitudes is that promise with room to spare, and it is about twelve
+/// thousand times tighter than the `n · ε` a plain `+=` earns over a hundred thousand samples.
+pub const BOUND: f64 = 4.0 * f64::EPSILON;
+
+/// A compensated (Kahan–Neumaier) accumulator: the sum a query computes values with.
+///
+/// The two float fields are the whole of the trick. `sum` is the running total, and `compensation`
+/// accumulates what each addition rounded away — the part of the addend that did not fit in `sum`
+/// after the addition. `value` is their sum, so the rounding is undone at the end rather than at
+/// every step, and the error a caller sees is the error of *one* addition rather than of a hundred
+/// thousand.
+///
+/// The shape is deliberately not an `f64` with extra methods: `Sum` is a value a pass carries
+/// through a window (`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s `Tally` is one), it is `Copy` so a pass can
+/// hand one out, and the count travels beside the sum because a mean is the one aggregate that
+/// needs both.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Default, PartialEq)]
+pub struct Sum {
+    /// The running total: what `+=` would have left behind.
+    sum: f64,
+    /// What the additions rounded away, accumulated instead of lost.
+    compensation: f64,
+    /// How many values have been added. An integer, and an exact one.
+    count: u64,
+}
+
